#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4aee0a3-1694-5a48-a21d-afd11f01889f" :knowledge-point/title "Graph Transformations Containing Vertical Translations and Stretches Only"}] :question/id #uuid "34af9714-5109-59b7-90cb-a85c52399759" :question/math-academy-id "e-4986" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=2f(x)+2$.\n\n![](images/24/243f8af2f2fe8e826866c228c5662baca03da66407e3cc7ba435ce80d3a9a7fe.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=2f(x)+2\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ce/cef2e07bd22dab7d69f51a0197960bcdd980d5412ee47237b387dad90a4655f9.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=2f(x) + 2$.\n\n![](images/a5/a55f6aecd289cc144e621d8ed7e298d51af50523d06b7455cbec9cb3fb58dd55.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100785 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23014f0a-c381-5997-9f41-2734d3102280" :knowledge-point/title "Graph Transformations Containing Horizontal Translations and Stretches Only"}] :question/id #uuid "7a7bda91-9647-57d1-b0d7-ad384d4a9753" :question/math-academy-id "e-7606" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=f\\left(2x+2\\right)$.\n\n![](images/76/76f9c7149797a0de54476f248f2ca0091bd585c5eb6d5a630b8262baa04e9fd6.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(2x + 2) = f(2(x + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=\\dfrac12$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/de/de6bb357ea6f3b8e7e797d914759f62f4b33629644af64a4a6cd66e7a969488d.png)\n\n- Then, translate it left by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f\\left(2\\left(x+1\\right)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/ba/ba287d0600a5c0536a36e492fe25e70540f0cc7087655d7a61c190ddd42ccca9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100786 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb2265f-df80-53ac-bcab-90c7925a4e86" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing Transformations Containing Horizontal and Vertical Stretches Only"}] :question/id #uuid "385bad53-d336-5749-8a77-c28dbfc4d963" :question/math-academy-id "e-7608" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Find the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$?\n\n![](images/6b/6b888df08fa6579335b74eb9cb0ead944298ea8b153765249855a8b304b36289.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=5f\\left(\\dfrac12x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = 2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/49/49614f79caf13a140495c49e8a77e69aac506d03baa23849ea3806118b9046d2.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5f\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/d4/d46eb3aa45d473518b0a33cfcb652e2fa6e7018afb81db0d60e85f38b6e6766e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100787 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2456098c-d6ed-5d85-a1a1-69ad54f02216" :knowledge-point/title "Other Cases of Graphing Transformations Containing Two Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "8b4eb466-41d1-5882-981e-0dca1dac1c32" :question/math-academy-id "e-7612" :question/problem "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Plot the graph of $y=f(3x)+1$?\n\n![](images/3e/3e5146f13fd0042d97a7905b9a07c14003531339267b972d044609611a0f81c9.png)" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f(3x)+1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x)$.\n\n![](images/0f/0fae441b0cb0b7bb7c5c8fa6e5de02cc32cf2eb0c138841eedfc6187e43b43fa.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(3x)+1$.\n\n![](images/b2/b2867f8f918683d9c66378b2beb87984a95924206e8830c7232a2802815d6737.png)"}]}
